WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:08.230
FELIPE HOFFA: Strata,
we need to talk.

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:10.360
Really.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:14.550
There is a huge debate, and
we need to put an end to it.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:20.090
And, I mean, people don't know
where to put their commas.

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:22.700
I'm talking about SQL.

00:00:22.700 --> 00:00:25.070
I'm sure most of
you must love SQL.

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:27.650
I love SQL.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:29.820
It's born in 1978.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:31.370
And it's the one language we--

00:00:31.370 --> 00:00:35.340
the best thing we have when
we want to analyze data.

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:37.840
But it's not perfect.

00:00:37.840 --> 00:00:42.145
Most of you must have learned
SQL with a sentence like this.

00:00:42.145 --> 00:00:45.270
This is how you get
one column of data.

00:00:45.270 --> 00:00:47.480
This is how you get two columns.

00:00:47.480 --> 00:00:49.120
Then you go for three.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:52.460
And suddenly, things don't work.

00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:54.460
What's wrong here?

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:56.700
That extra comma.

00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:58.150
Yes, you've been there.

00:00:58.150 --> 00:01:02.200
SQL will not allow you to
put a hanging comma there,

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:04.410
unlike any other
modern language.

00:01:04.410 --> 00:01:07.247
And this has driven people
to two different camps--

00:01:07.247 --> 00:01:09.330
there are people that put
their commas at the end;

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:12.240
people that put their
commas at the beginning.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.600
Who likes their
commas at the end?

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.320
Who likes their commas
at the beginning?

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.510
You monsters!

00:01:20.510 --> 00:01:22.520
That's ugly!

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:23.930
Well, I'm one of you.

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:25.970
I like to put my commas
at the beginning.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:27.810
I find it more productive.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:31.820
But it's hard to convince
people about this.

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:37.870
So I happen to work at Google
where we make decisions

00:01:37.870 --> 00:01:39.780
based on data.

00:01:39.780 --> 00:01:41.860
Also, I work for
Google Cloud platform

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:45.040
where we share our best
tools with everyone.

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:47.140
So things that we
use internally,

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.590
you are also able to use.

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:50.970
And one of them is BigQuery.

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:54.160
With BigQuery, we can analyze
not only terabytes of data

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:57.300
in seconds, we are
also able to share data

00:01:57.300 --> 00:02:02.010
within our teammates, within
third parties, publicly.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:06.130
And we love using it to
collaborate, to discuss,

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:09.699
to take the same data and
reach conclusions together.

00:02:09.699 --> 00:02:13.210
In this case, for example,
I have all of Github's data,

00:02:13.210 --> 00:02:14.670
started a query.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.480
And with a query
like this, I can

00:02:16.480 --> 00:02:20.560
see how many events
Github has seen

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:24.160
this year, 350 million events.

00:02:24.160 --> 00:02:26.080
I can join this table
with other tables.

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:30.370
I can bring in,
for example, count

00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:33.700
the number of distinct users,
active users this year.

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:36.970
And I can bring another table to
see where are they coming from,

00:02:36.970 --> 00:02:40.600
what are the most popular
countries on Github--

00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:44.860
the United States,
China, India--

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:48.700
which we would call
an unfair ranking.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:50.530
I mean, these are the
biggest countries.

00:02:50.530 --> 00:02:53.210
Of course, they
have the most users.

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:54.580
So let's bring in more tables.

00:02:54.580 --> 00:02:55.880
Let's bring in more data.

00:02:55.880 --> 00:03:00.250
Let's get the countries that
have the most Github users

00:03:00.250 --> 00:03:01.570
per capita.

00:03:01.570 --> 00:03:04.420
And now we have countries
like Iceland, Sweden, Norway,

00:03:04.420 --> 00:03:05.910
on top--

00:03:05.910 --> 00:03:08.300
cold countries.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:08.940
Or not.

00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:11.150
So now we can
bring in more data.

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:15.500
We can join this with the warm
weather data that we have in.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:18.620
And we can get a
chart like this,

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:21.590
which shows that, yes,
the coldest countries, up

00:03:21.590 --> 00:03:23.720
on the top left, are the
ones with the highest

00:03:23.720 --> 00:03:28.080
concentration of programmers,
the opposite on the other side.

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:32.430
But if you look at that
point on the top right,

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:35.170
that's Singapore.

00:03:35.170 --> 00:03:37.510
Yes, Singapore, you have
the highest concentration

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:41.240
of programmers on Github
within the hot countries.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.780
Congratulations.

00:03:43.780 --> 00:03:47.290
But let me get back
to our problem--

00:03:47.290 --> 00:03:49.580
where do we put our commas?

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:51.760
And this is not
tabs versus spaces.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.350
This really matters.

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:57.370
And I'm going to bring in all
code I can find on Github.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:01.330
I'm going to analyze
gigabytes of SQL code,

00:04:01.330 --> 00:04:03.910
and I will use it to
count the number of lines

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.460
that end with a comma,
start with a comma.

00:04:06.460 --> 00:04:12.130
And the results show that
most lines end with a comma.

00:04:12.130 --> 00:04:13.866
I lost.

00:04:13.866 --> 00:04:15.720
Or not.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:17.980
Because the real question
is, what projects

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.829
are more successful?

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:22.450
How do we measure that?

00:04:22.450 --> 00:04:24.900
The number of stars, the
number of stars last year,

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.990
the number of contributors, the
activity, et cetera, et cetera.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:31.010
We can define our success
metrics, and we can measure,

00:04:31.010 --> 00:04:32.690
and we can write a query.

00:04:32.690 --> 00:04:34.720
We can get the
results in seconds.

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.620
And it basically
says that projects

00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:41.140
that allow leading people
to write leading commas

00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:45.500
are double as productive
as the others.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:46.420
It's true.

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:49.710
You can go and measure it
in any measure you want.

00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:50.610
Thank you, yes.

00:04:50.610 --> 00:04:52.450
You were the
leading comma group.

00:04:52.450 --> 00:04:58.330
So my message here is that maybe
it will not stop our fights.

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:01.420
Because I'm sure I didn't
convince everyone here.

00:05:01.420 --> 00:05:04.300
Someone will ask about
correlation, causation.

00:05:04.300 --> 00:05:06.340
How do you-- are
you measuring this?

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:07.570
Why an average?

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:10.480
And the whole point is
that you can fight me.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:11.680
You can use the same tools.

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:16.270
You can use the same
queries and data,

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:18.340
and analyze it, and fight me.

00:05:18.340 --> 00:05:21.100
Some people we'll never
convince no matter how much data

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.670
we show them.

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:25.400
We call them industry analysts.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:26.680
Sorry.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:33.780
But, yes, at least we should
embrace data in this fight.

